task: actor-stop-drain
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/actor-stop-drain
worktree: ../okay-wt-drain

why: found by crdt-wire's gate and filed rather than chased. It is
  the only thing found today that looks like a CORRECTNESS defect
  rather than an inconvenience: `okay.actor.TestChildren`, "law: a
  parent's stop waits for its children to DRAIN", read 299 of 300.

what the test says, and why it is not a timing flake:

    (0 until 300).foreach(i => child.tell(i).runWith)
    parent.stop().runWith
    assertEquals(handled.get, 300)

  300 sends into a channel of capacity 1024, so every message is
  ACCEPTED; then `stop`, which the law says waits for accepted
  messages to be handled. There is NO clock in the test. 299 is the
  law failing, not a deadline missed.

  Contrast `resilience-timed-flake`, filed this morning, which
  asserts `elapsed >= 15 ms` — that one a loaded box can defeat with
  nothing wrong. This one it cannot.

THE HARD PART IS REPRODUCTION. It appeared once, in a full 90-module
  run, on a box carrying eight to twelve sibling sbt JVMs. Three
  isolated `testOnly` runs passed. So a bare re-run proves nothing in
  either direction, and the first job is a repro that FAILS ON
  DEMAND.

DISQUALIFYING EVIDENCE, registered before the work:

  - **no failing repro, no fix.** If I cannot make it fail on
    demand — by load, by more children and messages, or by a probe
    that widens the window deliberately — then I do not know what I
    am fixing, and any change I make is a guess wearing a diff. In
    that case the honest outcome is a sharper entry, not a patch.
  - a fix must make the ORIGINAL shape pass under the same load that
    broke it, not merely make the stress variant green.
  - the third possibility is that the LAW over-promises: `tell` is
    fire-and-forget, and if `stop` never promised to drain what was
    accepted after it began, the test is wrong and the spec should
    say so. Weakening the test is then the correct fix — but only
    after reading `stop` against `Channel`'s close, and said out
    loud, never by quietly adjusting a number.

predict: a window between `stop` deciding the child is done and the
  child's channel still holding an accepted message — the same shape
  as the sentinel/close races already recorded for Channel. Recorded
  so being wrong is visible.
